.TH CHMAP_INIT 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_init \- create a hash map into an already declared handle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_init(hm_name);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a hash map with the default allocator and the map's own hashing,
and assigns it to hm_name. The handle has to be declared first, with
chmap_declare(3) or chmap_declare_scoped(3). The key and value types are
not arguments, because the macro reads them from the companion type
variables that the declare macro created.

It is the create half of a declare-then-create pair, for a handle that
is declared in one place and created in another. When both happen
together, chmap_construct(3) creates the same map in one step.

The map picks its implementation from the declared key and value types,
as chmap_create_full(3) describes.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  The already declared map handle to create the map into
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro expands to a statement and is not an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Stops the program through _ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails; call
chmap_create(3) directly when a failure has to be handled instead of
being fatal
Uses CCOL_DEFAULT_INITIAL_BUCKET_ARRAY_SIZE (16) buckets
Assigning over a handle that already holds a map leaks that map, so
destroy the old one with chmap_destroy(3) first
chmap_destroy(3) destroys the handle, which a handle declared with
chmap_declare_scoped(3) doesn't need
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
chmap_declare(scores, char *, int);
chmap_init(scores);
int score = 42;
chmap_insert(scores, "alice", score);
chmap_destroy(scores);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_declare (3),
.BR chmap_declare_scoped (3),
.BR chmap_init_mp (3),
.BR chmap_init_ch (3),
.BR chmap_init_full (3),
.BR chmap_construct (3),
.BR chmap_create (3),
.BR chmap_destroy (3)
